==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|en|pædant||obsolete}}

===Etymology===
From {{bor|en|frm|pedant}}, {{m|frm|pedante}}, from {{der|en|it|pedante|t=a teacher, schoolmaster, pedant}}, associated with unrelated {{ncog|it|pedagogo|t=teacher, pedagogue}}. Compare {{cog|fr|pédant}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{enPR|pĕdʹənt}}, {{IPA|en|/ˈpɛdənt/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-pedant.wav|a=Southern England}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A person who [[make]]s an [[excessive]] or [[tedious]] [[show]] of their [[knowledge]], especially regarding [[rule]]s of [[vocabulary]] and [[grammar]].
# A person who is [[overly]] [[concern]]ed with [[formal]] rules and [[trivial]] [[point]]s of [[learn]]ing.
# {{lb|en|archaic}} A [[teacher]] or [[schoolmaster]].
#* {{RQ:Montaigne Florio Essayes|volume=1|chapter=24|passage=I have in my youth oftentimes beene vexed to see a '''Pedant''' [''tr. [[pedante]]''] brought in, in most of Italian comedies, for a vice or sport-maker, and the nicke-name of ''Magister'' to be of no better signification amongst us.}}

