# {{lb|en|rare|humorous}} To be or act as a [[#Noun|pedant]].
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Abraham Woodhead|title=Bodl., MS Clarendon 31|date=10 October 1648|section=folio 276, verso|passage={{...}} as any occasion of going behond{{sic|beyond}} the sea with sombody, or '''pedanting''' in some Gentlemans house, &c., for clergy-employment I will accept of none.}}<!-- https://archive.org/details/calendarofclaren01bodluoft/calendarofclaren01bodluoft/page/442/mode/1up https://archive.org/details/englishtraveller0000unse/page/223/mode/1up https://archive.org/details/englishtraveller0000unse/page/352/mode/1up https://ora.ox.ac.uk/objects/uuid:e78ff1f8-24d6-497e-90e7-0dc5023faf58/files/dpz50gw606 -->
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=George R. Hahn|title=Gangway for Homer|journal=w:Science Fiction Quarterly|issue=6|location=Holyoke, Mass.|publisher=w:Columbia Publications|month=spring|year=1942|page=125|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/Science_Fiction_Quarterly_n06_1942-Spring.Columbia/page/n124/mode/1up|column=2|oclc=22131523|passage=Tediously he '''pedanted''', hedging around concerning the Perfect State, eventually coming out into the open with his own private Perfect State plan.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Mark Barrowcliffe|chapter=How the lady gets sawn in half|title=Girlfriend 44|location=London|publisher=[[w:Headline Publishing Group|Headline Book Publishing]]|year=2000|page=223|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/girlfriend440000barr/page/223/mode/1up|isbn=0 7472 6814 2|passage=‘Most people in this country aren’t Christian, the standard package should not be Christian,’ Gerrard '''pedanted'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Mark Dahl|chapter=18|title=Covenant Betrayed: Revelations of the Sixties, the Best of Time; the Worst of Time|volume_plain=book 2 (Despair and Dessent)|location=Bloomington, Ind.|publisher=w:AuthorHouse|date=25 February 2005|page=149|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=fRkoa6BHC9QC&pg=PA149|isbn=1-4208-0568-1|passage=Jim’s mother pestered him daily for a month to come home for a talk. At first he rejected the offer, knowing what to expect, dear old dad would dance around the room '''pedanting''' about some shit that didn’t make sense anyway.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Ira Nayman|chapter=Carrie-Anne’s Crew|chapterurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=yKUlDwAAQBAJ&pg=PT66|title=The Multiverse is a Nice Place to Visit, But I Wouldn’t Want to Live There|series=Transdimensional Authority|seriesvolume=5|location=Dartford, Kent|publisher=Elsewhen Press|year=2017|isbn=978-1-911409-09-0|passage=“Okay,” she said to the investigators, “how can I help you?” / “You, uhh, wouldn’t happen to have seven bodies lying around, would you?” Bao Bai-Leung, feeling a little ridiculous, asked. “Maybe in…your closet?” / “As I under stand human Anna to my,”{{sic}} Blarcch Beletchian '''pedanted''' at them, “there is not enough room for bodies to lie in a small space like a closet. Unless they were midgets like this person, or their limbs were detachable, or the closet was a gateway to eleven-dimensional space, or –” / “Oh, great!” TOM put in. “We got a literalist, here!”}}
#* {{RQ:Guardian|author=David Steele|title=Why do pedants '''pedant'''?|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20170530084000/https://www.theguardian.com/science/brain-flapping/2017/may/30/why-do-pedants-pedant|date=30 May 2017|passage=So, why do pedants '''pedant'''? We don’t really know, but some tangential studies infer it’s to do with a mixture of personality, status-signalling and group identification.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Alex Beeton|chapter=‘A shame to bee out of a prison, or in a Felloship’: Cooperation and Education|title=‘Not Infected with the Venime of the Times’: The Rump Parliament and Places of Learning, 1649-53|url=https://ora.ox.ac.uk/objects/uuid:e78ff1f8-24d6-497e-90e7-0dc5023faf58|others=thesis submitted for the degree of Doctor of Philosophy|location=Oxford, Oxfordshire|publisher=w:University of Oxford|year=2022|page=212|passage=When one Oxford fellow was ejected in 1648 he immediately wrote to his royalist patron seeking employment ‘going behond [''sic''] the sea with sombody, or '''pedanting''' in some Gentlemans house’.{{sup|128}} Whether he succeeded or not is not known, but others certainly ended up ‘'''pedanting'''’.}}

